meta {
  name: Node A - Create Peer for B
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: http://{{adminA}}/peers
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "id": "04978037-26b8-4b4d-854a-278ed7548959",
    "name": "Node B",
    "assetCode": "USD",
    "assetScale": 2,
    "ilpStaticAddress": "g.rafiki",
    "http": {
      "outgoing": {
        "endpoint": "http://open-payments-resource-service-b:3002/ilp",
        "authToken": "two-node-token"
      }
    }
  }
}

script:post-response {
  const body = res.getBody();
  if (body?.id) {
    bru.setVar("peerId", body.id);
  }
}

tests {
  test("created or already exists", function () {
    expect([201, 409]).to.include(res.getStatus());
  });
}

docs {
  Creates the peer on **Node A** that represents **Node B**. During a remote
  quote, Node A resolves this peer by the incoming payment's destination ILP
  address, then dispatches ILP packets to its `http.outgoing.endpoint`.

  About the two ILP values:

  - `ilpStaticAddress` (`g.rafiki`) - this must match the stubbed prefix the
    payments BC uses; it is NOT arbitrary. Node B's remote incoming payment
    resolves to a destination ILP address of `g.rafiki.<incomingPaymentId>`
    (see `remote-payment-intent-adapter.ts`), and Node A strips the last
    segment and looks the peer up in PAL by the `g.rafiki` prefix
    (`payment-intent/aggregate.ts`). Any other value fails the remote quote
    with "No peer found for ILP address ...".
  - `http.outgoing.endpoint` (`.../ilp`) - still a placeholder: the ILP HTTP
    ingress route is not implemented on any service yet, and the connector
    loops back, so packets are not actually delivered here. It uses the
    Docker-internal service name so Node A would reach Node B over the shared
    `rafiki-v2` network once a real transport exists.

  `id` is hardcoded so re-running Setup returns 409 (idempotent) rather than
  creating a duplicate peer.
}
